% Installing and retiring the paginated PDF writer repeatedly must not spend
% global memory per document.
%
% The shared record outlives any one document and names what it holds, so a
% container made for each document is held for as long as the record is: a
% context that renders one document per job grows without bound. The
% writer's across-page maps therefore belong to the record, made once with
% it, and a document restarts the counters that key them instead of
% replacing them.
%
% The output file comes from the command line, so stdout carries only the
% verdict. The bound is generous against the fixed per-document cost (the name
% string and the counters) and far below what a fresh set of maps costs.

% COLLECTION: measured insensitive to collection frequency -- the same
% result with no collection at all and with one every two thousand
% bytes.

/globalused { globalvmstatus pop exch pop } bind def

/cycle {
    << /OutputDevice /pdfwrite /PageSize [200 200] >> setpagedevice
    0 0 moveto 50 0 rlineto 0 50 rlineto closepath fill
    showpage
    % retiring the writer finalises the document, so the next install starts a
    % new one and builds a new device
    << /OutputDevice /null >> setpagedevice
} bind def

% the first documents pay one-off costs -- the writer's own load, the shared
% record, the maps themselves -- which are not per-document
0 1 9 { pop cycle } for

globalused
0 1 49 { pop cycle } for
globalused exch sub 50 div

dup (  global memory per document: ) print 20 string cvs print ( bytes\n) print
1024 lt
{ (SUCCESS\n) print }
{ (FAIL: a document spends global memory the record goes on naming\n) print }
ifelse
